  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "What is the smallest possible distance between the points $(b,2b)$ and $(3,0),$ where $b$ is a real number? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe smallest possible distance is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(b)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(b) & ={(b-3)}^{2}+{(2b-0)}^{2} \\\\  & =(b-3)^{2}+(2b)^{2} \\\\  & =({b}^{2}-6b+9)+(4{b}^{2}) \\\\  & =5{b}^{2}-6b+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{\u2032}(b).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{S}^{\u2032}(b)=10b-6.\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(b)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{\u2032}(b)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{\u2032}(b) & =0 \\\\ 10b-6 & =0 \\\\ b & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{\u2033}\\,(\\frac{3}{5})=10>0.$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $b=\\frac{3}{5}.$ \n\nSince $S(b)$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $b=\\frac{3}{5}$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{5})}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S\\,(\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-6(\\frac{3}{5})+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{5}-\\frac{18}{5}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{36}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the smallest possible distance between the points $(b,2b)$ and $(3,0)$ is $\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$",
